title: Numbers: a 4-bit adder
mode: vhdl
chapter: Buses and numbers
check: behaves
---- intro
Four full adders in a row add two 4-bit numbers, carry going from each to the next. VHDL can build that chain for you: treat the bits as a number and use `+`.

The `numeric_std` library (in the starter) has `unsigned`, a bus read as a number:

- `unsigned(a)` reads the bits of `a` as a number
- `resize(x, 5)` makes a number 5 bits wide
- `std_logic_vector(x)` turns the number back into bits for an output

The answer can be up to 15 + 15 = 30, so `s` has **5** bits: the fifth is the last carry.
---- task
Add the 4-bit numbers `a` and `b`; `s` is the 5-bit sum.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;
use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity adder4 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    b : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    s : out std_logic_vector(4 downto 0)
  );
end adder4;

architecture rtl of adder4 is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;
use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity adder4 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    b : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    s : out std_logic_vector(4 downto 0)
  );
end adder4;

architecture rtl of adder4 is
begin
    s <= std_logic_vector(resize(unsigned(a), 5) + unsigned(b));
end rtl;
---- hint1
Make `a` 5 bits wide before adding, so the last carry is not lost.
---- hint2
`s <= std_logic_vector(resize(unsigned(a), 5) + unsigned(b));`
---- solution
`s <= std_logic_vector(resize(unsigned(a), 5) + unsigned(b));` Open the adder on the right to see the full adders inside it.
---- done
One `+`, and Wire builds the adder you would otherwise wire from full adders. Engineers write `+` and let the tools make the gates.
